Bootstrap: docker
From: rockylinux:9

# NOTE on provenance (departs from this project's usual Pattern 0-4 install
# patterns — see CLAUDE.md): BV-BRC-Go-SDK is a 101-command Go CLI suite
# (p3-*), not a single-tool bioinformatics package. It has no PyPI/bioconda
# presence and no Python packaging, so this project's automated
# create_def_file.sh (Python/bioconda-oriented) was skipped entirely — this
# .def was hand-authored instead, adapting the install logic straight from
# upstream's own tools/build-apptainer.sh (see
# https://github.com/olsonanl/BV-BRC-Go-SDK/blob/main/build-apptainer.sh),
# per CLAUDE.md lesson 4. Upstream's binaries are fully static
# (CGO_ENABLED=0), so the container only needs a CA bundle plus the
# release tarball's bin/ dropped onto PATH.
#
# The release tarball bound in below (%files) was downloaded from
# upstream's official v2.0.11 GitHub Release and its sha256 was verified
# against the release's own published bvbrc-cli-2.0.11-checksums.sha256
# before this build ever ran (upstream does not publish a checksum for its
# prebuilt .sif assets — only for the tarball/installer artifacts — which
# is why this build extracts from the checksummed tarball rather than
# re-hosting upstream's prebuilt .sif directly).

%files
    /share/brc/sdweave2/.apptainer/bvbrc_src/bvbrc-cli-2.0.11-linux-amd64.tar.gz /opt/bvbrc-cli.tar.gz

%labels
    Maintainer sdweave2@ncsu.edu
    Source https://github.com/olsonanl/BV-BRC-Go-SDK
    Version 2.0.11

%help
    BV-BRC-Go-SDK — Go language implementation of the BV-BRC CLI toolkit,
    providing 101 p3-* command-line tools (mirroring the Perl p3_cli suite)
    for programmatic and command-line access to BV-BRC bioinformatics data
    and services (genomes, features, subsystems, job submission, etc.),
    plus importable Go libraries (api, appservice, auth, workspace).

    This is a multi-command toolkit, not a single tool — %runscript passes
    its arguments straight through to whichever p3-* command you name, so
    every command in the suite is reachable the same way.

    Usage:
        apptainer run BV-BRC-Go-SDK-2.0.11.sif <p3-command> [options]
        apptainer exec BV-BRC-Go-SDK-2.0.11.sif <p3-command> [options]

    Examples:
        apptainer run BV-BRC-Go-SDK-2.0.11.sif p3-login --help
        apptainer run BV-BRC-Go-SDK-2.0.11.sif p3-all-genomes --help
        apptainer run BV-BRC-Go-SDK-2.0.11.sif p3-submit-genome-assembly --help

    See https://github.com/olsonanl/BV-BRC-Go-SDK for the full command
    reference (101 p3-* commands under cmd/).

%post -c /bin/bash
    set -e
    export DEBIAN_FRONTEND=noninteractive

    # Hazel bind-mount points
    mkdir -p /rs1 /share /home /usr/local/usrapps

    # Static Go binaries only need a CA bundle for TLS against the BV-BRC
    # data API. rockylinux:9's base image already ships a working
    # ca-certificates package (its own dnf metadata fetches above already
    # succeeded over TLS using it), so no dnf/rpm work is needed for CA
    # certs at all — and none is safe to do here: any `dnf install/upgrade
    # ca-certificates` resolves to an *upgrade* transaction against the
    # already-installed package, and that transaction's RPM payload unpack
    # itself fails under Apptainer's rootless/fakeroot build sandbox
    # ("Error unpacking rpm package ca-certificates...") — confirmed
    # empirically across two build attempts (2026-08-06): plain install
    # failed, and retrying with `--setopt=tsflags=nocontexts` (which only
    # skips SELinux context labeling, not the unpack step) failed
    # identically. This is the RPM-world counterpart to CLAUDE.md lesson
    # 7's dpkg/SELinux failure — avoiding the RPM transaction entirely is
    # the fix, not adding more flags to it.
    #
    # Instead, fetch curl's official, regularly-updated CA bundle (the
    # Mozilla root program, repackaged by the curl project) straight to
    # disk — bypassing dnf/rpm so the unpack bug can't fire — and point
    # Go's TLS stack at it explicitly via SSL_CERT_FILE (Go's crypto/x509
    # on Unix honors this env var as an override to the system trust
    # store). Fetched over the same already-verified-working TLS path the
    # base image's own dnf calls just used above.
    curl -fsSL https://curl.se/ca/cacert.pem -o /etc/pki/tls/certs/ca-bundle-current.crt
    dnf clean all

    # Extract the (sha256-verified before build) release tarball's bin/ ->
    # /usr/local/bin, already on PATH. Matches upstream's own
    # build-apptainer.sh %post logic.
    tar -xzf /opt/bvbrc-cli.tar.gz -C /usr/local --strip-components=1
    rm /opt/bvbrc-cli.tar.gz

%environment
    export DEBIAN_FRONTEND=noninteractive
    export LC_ALL=C

%runscript
    # Passthrough, not a single bareword command — deliberate deviation
    # from this project's usual single-tool %runscript convention. This
    # is a 101-command suite (p3-*); `exec "$@"` lets
    # `apptainer run image.sif <any-p3-command> ...` work for all of them,
    # matching upstream's own build-apptainer.sh choice.
    exec "$@"

%test
    # p3-login is a representative smoke test, not "the" command. Go CLI
    # help output doesn't reliably exit 0 across frameworks (see CLAUDE.md
    # lesson 8), so this checks for "usage" in the combined output instead
    # of trusting --help's exit code.
    p3-login --help 2>&1 | grep -qi usage
